module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.census_rows_read_the_loaded_population

import std.types { String, NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { BelowCeiling, FoundOnRung, RecurringFailureMode, RungRead }
import gunbc.guarantee_rung { MechanicallyPreventable, Mitigatable }
import gunbc.rung_climb { ClimbableButUnbuilt }

// REGISTERED BY THE SHARD-CONTEXT DEFECT (Manager 1, 2026-10-21, msg_dfb4289f; strengthened by
// clever-carp-254's fleet rows at run 39622052948 and sunny-bee-153's artifact read on branch
// scratch/census-nonhermetic-evidence). A native census row must be a function of the MODULE and its
// CLOSURE. It was not: whether a byte-identical module's ambient primitive name bound depended on which
// SUBJECTS the run named -- the row read the loaded POPULATION. The FIRST fix (session branch, October
// 2026, #14710 as first written) unioned the contract module into every population at the one shared
// scoped ingest; it was executed WRONG and reversed at head (see THE REVERSAL receipt): the census began
// binding through a contract the native route refuses at, spreading the instrument-hides-a-wall state to
// the route instead of removing it. The surviving fix closes the OTHER direction: neither acquisition path
// admits the ambient primitive contracts as a resolution authority -- the census's entries are THE SUBJECTS
// EXACTLY (fn native_census_scoped_entries, v2.compiler.source_root_eval), so the population is a pure
// function of the subjects and the census's first fatal is byte-identical to the route's. This row carries
// the class, both fixes, and the executed measurements that settled the direction.

data census_rows_read_the_loaded_population: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "census_rows_read_the_loaded_population" as NonEmptyStr,

  // The rung is READABLE for this class, so it is read (review 84236 / side chat RC, October 2026):
  // a discriminating RED run by execution on the real files (a module's ambient primitive rows read
  // unbound when its population does not carry the contract module -- the two-population flip below,
  // re-recorded as the RED of the REVERSED fix), a positive repaired execution (one binary, two
  // subject populations, the same module's rows byte-identical, and the census's first fatal
  // byte-identical to the route's), and the same comparison enrolled as a regression control. CURRENT
  // is Mitigatable, not higher: the RED and the enrolled control catch a recurrence when the fleet or
  // the claim suite EXECUTES, and the two identity claims in v2.test.claim.census_scoped_acquisition
  // CI-refuse anyone re-appending a module the subjects did not name -- but a new scoped route could
  // still bypass native_census_scoped_entries and nothing at build time refuses that yet, so the class
  // is not mechanically prevented. CEILING is MechanicallyPreventable and the blocker is unbuilt work,
  // not a grounding: hoisting the acquisition to one ingest+resolve per run keyed by content identity
  // -- the same hoist that ends the ~110-minute per-round re-read at the fleet's base profile -- makes
  // re-entry impossible rather than CI-refused. Trigger capability: the capability of ingesting and
  // resolving the census population once per run, memoized by content identity, executed check = the
  // fleet log's census Filesystem.Read count for the shared closure (once vs 10 x 22 now).
  rung: RungRead { found_at: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable }, standing: BelowCeiling { current: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable }, ceiling: MechanicallyPreventable, blocker: ClimbableButUnbuilt, trigger_capability: "the capability of ingesting and resolving the census population once per census run, memoized by content identity, so no fold re-enters the filesystem layer per round and no scoped route can carry its own population read -- the fleet log's census-subject Filesystem.Read count (once vs 10 x 22) is the executed check." } },

  receipts: [
    "THE FAILURE MODE: the resolver's ambient primitive-authority tier (v2.compiler.resolve resolve_atom_primitive_authority) binds an unimported spelling -- first, contains, map_get, starts_with, join, split, replace, string_length, fold, keys, values -- by looking up a contract row (std.primitives.<name>_contract) in the symbol index of the modules the run LOADED. While the scoped census loaded exactly the named subjects' closures, whether such a name bound was a fact about the invocation's subject list, not about the module: the same module, same binary, emitted binding_partial ... ambient:primitive_authority std.primitives.first_contract in one run and binding_unbound in another, because std.primitives rode the first run's closures and not the second's. A census consumer comparing base against shards then reads the difference as a behavior change of the module and refuses a merge that changed nothing -- which is exactly how this blocked //gunbc/instruments:namespace-wave-binding-diff's positive merge for #14324.",

    "THE TELL: a binding row for a module whose bytes are byte-identical between two runs differs in binding state (partial<->unbound), the flipped names are all ambient primitive spellings, and the two runs loaded different module populations (check the census_run_comparable population field, or the run's subject list). The module's own imports are unchanged and do not name the substrate -- ambient resolution is import-free by design, which is what makes the population an invisible dependency.",

    "EXECUTED REPRODUCTION, one binary (emitted driver, sha256 2742801a..., head 3cd5c856e48f, instrument route //gunbc/instruments:v2-native-census-scoped), the subject list the ONLY variable: census-scoped //std/fermi:all prints 'binding_unbound std.fermi first 72:20' while census-scoped //std/fermi:all //std/primitives:all prints 'binding std.fermi first 0 ambient:primitive_authority std.primitives.first_contract 72:20'; the uri_path pair (//extdeps/uri_path:all plus/minus //std/primitives:all) shows the same flip on 'contains' at 82:15, aggregate binding_unbound 157 vs 58. THE FLEET ROWS (clever-carp-254, fermi/ at scratch branch 359d18ef): base tree 02b9486ad13 vs head 80fe982d7f37, std.fermi byte-identical, same instrument revision acb862a78f625330-d48b9c56c12811d2 -- exactly a 3-row delta (binding_partial first ... first_contract 72:20 + its two module-count rollups, versus binding_unbound) across base (1,948-module population) and all four shards (165 modules); the four shards agree with each other as sets. sunny-bee-153's EVIDENCE.txt: std.primitives has 0 rows in the shard population vs 374 at base, and every untouched-module difference is one of ~25 primitive spellings flipping partial->unbound.",

    "THE EARLIEST BOUNDARY (DESIGN 6b): not the resolver tier -- reading the loaded index is what a namespace lookup IS. The boundary is the ACQUISITION. THE FIRST FIX AT THAT BOUNDARY WAS EXECUTED WRONG, and the executed wrongness is the receipt: appending the contract module to every scoped population (#14710 as first written, head 533defac4713) made the census load a resolution authority the NATIVE ROUTE never loads (fn native_lane_candidate_entries had no such append). Measured on the D1 matrix (same binaries, both acquisition paths): with the append, the route's parse-label first fatal sat at resolve_reason_unbound_symbol @ dag/std/algebra.dag bytes 5195..5204 (list_push) while the substrate-fed census bound list_push THROUGH std.primitives.list_push_contract; the follow-on join (#14832, moving the roster onto the route) proved which side moves -- the route's first fatal then MOVED to Map @ bytes 73768..73771, i.e. the route began resolving list_push through a contract row, which the v2 native ruling forbids (ambient contracts retire; list_push gets its one DECLARED home, #14751). A probe showed a population entry binds ONLY contract-registry rows: adding std.types to the population left bare Map unbound (scratch/qc503-substrate-types @ 8461234f66ea). And the census claims' green was BORROWED from the append: with the roster gone the claim module itself cannot prepare, because its std.algebra import's body uses ambient list_push (probe scratch/qc503-claims-only @ 4741a1f271c9, all members refused at prepare). THE SURVIVING FIX IS THE OPPOSITE DIRECTION: neither acquisition path admits the ambient contracts; native_census_scoped_entries returns THE SUBJECTS EXACTLY (identity, argv order, nothing appended), so the population is a pure function of the subjects and the census matches THE ROUTE. The RED that keeps this honest is two-part: build-time, the identity claims in v2.test.claim.census_scoped_acquisition (the_census_admits_no_ambient_resolution_authority, a_subject_list_gets_exactly_itself_whatever_it_reaches) RED the moment anyone re-appends a module the subjects did not name; executed, the census's first fatal must be BYTE-IDENTICAL to the route's on the same subject -- both resolve_reason_unbound_symbol @ dag/std/algebra.dag bytes 5195..5204 (list_push) -- with census rows reporting list_push unbound (run IDs in the PR receipt).",

    "THE OPEN HALF, recorded here so it is not lost: the fleet ALSO reported per-module observation flips (gunbc.host_names.gen_z and extdeps.bmc.ipmi_boot_selection reading ObservationComplete in some shard ranges and ObservationIncomplete with reason native_census_infer_stops_at_first_fatal in others, same binary). That pairing does not exist at main: the reason is minted only in the InferRefused arm of native_census_infer_completeness and cannot pair with outcome=resolve_refused, and a bisect over the shard-B subject prefix (223, 112, 56, 28, 14, 7, 3, 1 subjects, and gen_z alone) shows the printed observation never flips on any population -- so the report is an unverified transcription (its author's own caution, relayed by deep-hawk-258) or a reading of the cause-group STAGE rather than the observation field. A second population-read candidate -- the emitted walk threading context = step.context.clone() across modules (src/v1/05_emit_rust.dag) so module N resolves against module N-1's published closure -- is real as code but produced no observation flip in the bisect. NEITHER is claimed as fixed. The next rung: a fleet re-run on the fixed identity (base + shards + positive merge/4) either reproduces an observation flip -- at which point the per-module context threading becomes the suspect and its own trigger fires -- or retires the report; the per-round census re-ingest measured by clever-carp-254 (22 clusters x ~5m07s in the binding-diff walk, population-independent) is the same open half's cost face and stays open until the round count in a fleet log drops to one ingest of the shared closure.",

    "THE COST, one sentence: one full positive-merge chain (#14324) plus three sessions' fleet hours went into bisecting a 'behavior change' in a module whose bytes never changed, while the only variable -- the subject list -- sat outside the census record's comparison.",

    "THE PHASE MARKERS, deferred with a lane and this tracking row (review 84222's advisory, October 2026): the four eprintln! phase markers in the hand-written seed Rust src/v1/stage0/src/target_invocation_host.rs run_namespace_wave_binding_diff (base-census start/done, head-census start/done) change no decision; they make the two silent census blocks legible in a runner log, so a live census is distinguishable from a hang (Lane 3 condition c), and they are admitted because they serve the v2 self-host program (gunbc.v1.v1_maintenance_standing v1_seed_standing). The lane is v1-hand-queue-drain -- gunbc.namespace_wave_binding_diff_seed_growth's own owning_dissolution_lane -- and this row is the tracking row. They leave the seed when the binding-diff producer dispatch is emitted rather than hand-mirrored, the same trigger that retires the seed_growth justification they live inside.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
